\section{Encrypting}{


Encryption needs only public material, so a party handed that
material at setup encrypts entirely on its own, with nothing to
consult and no one to ask — which is what makes \code{\link[=contribute]{contribute()}} a
purely local computation. Methods are registered on whatever holds
the public material:
\itemize{
\item a \link{Site} encrypts with the parameters it was given when it was
wired, so \code{encrypt(site, value)} needs nothing besides the site
itself;
\item \link{OpenFHEParams} encrypts under a bundle held directly, which is
what a party reads back from a site with \code{\link[=site_params]{site_params()}};
\item the frozen \link{PaillierParams} and \link{PaillierPublicKey} encrypt under
the Paillier scheme.
}

For the \code{openfhe} backends the encoding follows whatever the
context was built for, read back from the context itself: packed
reals under CKKS, packed integers under BFV and BGV. The exact
schemes reject a value they cannot represent rather than round it;
see \link{OpenFHEParams}.

There is deliberately no method on \link{Master}, and \code{public_params()}
is deliberately not exported. An encryption entry point taking a
master would advertise a privilege that does not exist, and would
invite site-side code to reach back to a coordinator for something
it was already given. A site is autonomous once configured.
}

\section{Decrypting}{


Decryption is the asymmetric half of the pair, and that asymmetry is
the point: it takes either secret material or the standing to
convene every site, while encryption takes neither. Methods are
registered on the decrypting party:
\itemize{
\item \link{CKKSMaster} decrypts with the secret key it holds;
\item \link{ThresholdMaster} holds no key material at all and recovers a
value by asking each site for a partial decryption through
\code{\link[=partial_decrypt]{partial_decrypt()}} and fusing the results, so no party — the
master included — can decrypt alone;
\item the frozen \link{PaillierMaster} and \link{PaillierPrivateKey} decrypt
under the Paillier scheme.
}

The master methods take \code{len}, the number of packed slots to
return, defaulting to \code{1}.
}

\section{Return semantics}{


The methods return deliberately different types, because the
encodings differ:
\itemize{
\item \link{CKKSMaster} and \link{ThresholdMaster} return a \code{numeric} of length
\code{len}, decoded for whatever scheme the context was built for.
\item \link{PaillierMaster} returns the scalar real its protocol
accumulated.
\item a \link{PaillierCiphertext} under a \link{PaillierPrivateKey} gives a
\link[gmp:bigz]{gmp::bigz} in \verb{[0, n)}. This preserves raw mod-\code{n} arithmetic;
callers wanting signed integers should re-center themselves
(\code{if (m > n/2) m - n}).
\item a \link{PaillierEncryptedReal} gives a \code{numeric} in \verb{(-n/2, n/2)}. The
method re-centers the raw mod-\code{n} residues so that negative real
numbers and running totals that cross zero round-trip correctly.
See \link{PaillierEncryptedReal} for the full convention.
}
}

\section{Argument names}{


The generics belong to \code{openfhe.R}, and their argument names follow
the OpenFHE C++ signatures: \code{encrypt(key, pt, ...)} for
\code{Encrypt(publicKey, plaintext)}, and \code{decrypt(ct, key, ...)} for
\code{Decrypt(ciphertext, privateKey)}. S7 requires every method to use
the generic's names for the arguments it dispatches on, so those
are the names here too, and in a call on a protocol actor they read
by position: in \code{encrypt(site, value)}, \code{key} is the site and \code{pt}
the value; in \code{decrypt(master, ct, len)}, \code{ct} is the master and
\code{key} the encrypted value. Every call in this package and its
vignettes is positional, so the names are never written out.
}

